\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces What keeps the system solvable, on the benchmark configuration ($\varepsilon =2$\,mrad, $\gamma =3$, $\delta =1$, clean events, three repetitions per point). (a) The measured extremes of the spectrum of $A$ against the sufficient condition: the Gershgorin floor $\qopname  \relax m{min}_i(A_{ii}-R_i)=s-d_{\qopname  \relax m{max}}$ (grey) reaches zero once some segment first acquires four compatible neighbours, near $T=400$, while the true $\lambda _{\qopname  \relax m{min}}$ (green) stays between $0.8$ and $2.4$ all the way to $T=1000$ and four million segments. The guarantee lapses; the matrix stays positive definite anyway, because it is overwhelmingly sparse. (b) The condition number that follows. (c) The mechanism: the largest off-diagonal row sum saturates at $4=s$, which is what makes the bound vacuous, while the mean row sum falls towards zero as the isolated population grows.\relax }}{7}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:spectrum}{{1}{7}{What keeps the system solvable, on the benchmark configuration ($\varepsilon =2$\,mrad, $\gamma =3$, $\delta =1$, clean events, three repetitions per point). (a) The measured extremes of the spectrum of $A$ against the sufficient condition: the Gershgorin floor $\min _i(A_{ii}-R_i)=s-d_{\max }$ (grey) reaches zero once some segment first acquires four compatible neighbours, near $T=400$, while the true $\lambda _{\min }$ (green) stays between $0.8$ and $2.4$ all the way to $T=1000$ and four million segments. The guarantee lapses; the matrix stays positive definite anyway, because it is overwhelmingly sparse. (b) The condition number that follows. (c) The mechanism: the largest off-diagonal row sum saturates at $4=s$, which is what makes the bound vacuous, while the mean row sum falls towards zero as the isolated population grows.\relax }{figure.caption.2}{}}
\newlabel{eq:gammaknob}{{5}{7}{The Hamiltonian}{equation.2.5}{}}
\newlabel{eq:dpform}{{6}{7}{The Hamiltonian}{equation.2.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The positive-definiteness condition, drawn. (a) Because $A$ has constant diagonal $s$ and $0/1$ off-diagonals, every Gershgorin disc is centred at $s$ with radius equal to that segment's number of compatible neighbours $d_i$; the sufficient condition $\gamma +\delta >d_{\qopname  \relax m{max}}$ is exactly the statement that no disc reaches the origin. (b) Why the condition is so much weaker than the truth: the degree distribution is overwhelmingly concentrated at $d_i=0$ --- $98.6\%$ even under heavy noise --- and only eleven segments in $157{,}609$ reach $d_i=s$. (c) The control knob. Equation\nobreakspace  {}\textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:gammaknob}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} makes $\lambda _{\qopname  \relax m{min}}(A)$ a unit-slope line in $\gamma $, so the self-penalty needed for positive definiteness can be read off exactly (triangles); $\gamma =3$ clears it at every configuration used here.\relax }}{8}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gershgorin}{{2}{8}{The positive-definiteness condition, drawn. (a) Because $A$ has constant diagonal $s$ and $0/1$ off-diagonals, every Gershgorin disc is centred at $s$ with radius equal to that segment's number of compatible neighbours $d_i$; the sufficient condition $\gamma +\delta >d_{\max }$ is exactly the statement that no disc reaches the origin. (b) Why the condition is so much weaker than the truth: the degree distribution is overwhelmingly concentrated at $d_i=0$ --- $98.6\%$ even under heavy noise --- and only eleven segments in $157{,}609$ reach $d_i=s$. (c) The control knob. Equation~\eqref {eq:gammaknob} makes $\lambda _{\min }(A)$ a unit-slope line in $\gamma $, so the self-penalty needed for positive definiteness can be read off exactly (triangles); $\gamma =3$ clears it at every configuration used here.\relax }{figure.caption.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Where the angular acceptance comes from. (a) The geometry of one measured kink: the particle takes a scattering kick in each projection at the shared plane, and all three hits are recorded with an independent position error, so the measured kink is the second difference of three smeared coordinates (schematic; the true angles are a few milliradians and are drawn hugely exaggerated). (b) The resulting three-dimensional kink is Rayleigh-distributed, not Gaussian: the histogram is $3600$ true kinks measured on six generated events at the heavy configuration ($\sigma _{\rm  scatt}=10^{-4}$, $\sigma _{\rm  res}=20\,\mu $m, $T=200$), the curve is Rayleigh($\sigma _p$) with $\sigma _p$ from the closed form and no fit, and the vertical is the acceptance eq.\nobreakspace  {}\textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:epsilon}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} sets, which by construction discards $e^{-9}=1.2\times 10^{-4}$ of true continuations.\relax }}{9}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:epsilon}{{3}{9}{Where the angular acceptance comes from. (a) The geometry of one measured kink: the particle takes a scattering kick in each projection at the shared plane, and all three hits are recorded with an independent position error, so the measured kink is the second difference of three smeared coordinates (schematic; the true angles are a few milliradians and are drawn hugely exaggerated). (b) The resulting three-dimensional kink is Rayleigh-distributed, not Gaussian: the histogram is $3600$ true kinks measured on six generated events at the heavy configuration ($\sigma _{\rm scatt}=10^{-4}$, $\sigma _{\rm res}=20\,\mu $m, $T=200$), the curve is Rayleigh($\sigma _p$) with $\sigma _p$ from the closed form and no fit, and the vertical is the acceptance eq.~\eqref {eq:epsilon} sets, which by construction discards $e^{-9}=1.2\times 10^{-4}$ of true continuations.\relax }{figure.caption.4}{}}
\newlabel{eq:epsilon}{{7}{9}{The Hamiltonian}{equation.2.7}{}}
