\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces From the detector to the spectrum, for four clusters. Left to right: the segments as they lie in the detector (green true, red false); the compatibility graph they form, one vertex per segment and one edge per compatible head-to-tail pair; the block of $A=sI-C$ that graph contributes, drawn at a size proportional to the number of segments; and the eigenvalues of that block. A solid line is a \emph  {bright} mode, one the uniform bias $\mathbf  {b}=\delta \mathbf  {1}$ actually excites; an open circle is a \emph  {dark} mode, which carries an eigenvalue but no amplitude (section\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:toy:modes}), with its multiplicity marked where lines coincide. The dotted green verticals are the four true-track lines and the dotted grey vertical is $\lambda =s$.\relax }}{12}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:correspondence}{{4}{12}{From the detector to the spectrum, for four clusters. Left to right: the segments as they lie in the detector (green true, red false); the compatibility graph they form, one vertex per segment and one edge per compatible head-to-tail pair; the block of $A=sI-C$ that graph contributes, drawn at a size proportional to the number of segments; and the eigenvalues of that block. A solid line is a \emph {bright} mode, one the uniform bias $\mathbf {b}=\delta \mathbf {1}$ actually excites; an open circle is a \emph {dark} mode, which carries an eigenvalue but no amplitude (section~\ref {sec:toy:modes}), with its multiplicity marked where lines coincide. The dotted green verticals are the four true-track lines and the dotted grey vertical is $\lambda =s$.\relax }{figure.caption.6}{}}
\citation{trackhhl}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces How one lost hit breaks a track. Top: the five hits of a true track, with the lost one drawn as an open red circle and the segments it kills dashed. Bottom: the cluster that survives, and its exact activation. Losing an interior hit is worse than losing an end one, and losing the middle hit is worst of all: it leaves two one-segment stubs at $0.250$, indistinguishable by amplitude from an isolated fake.\relax }}{13}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fragmentation}{{5}{13}{How one lost hit breaks a track. Top: the five hits of a true track, with the lost one drawn as an open red circle and the segments it kills dashed. Bottom: the cluster that survives, and its exact activation. Losing an interior hit is worse than losing an end one, and losing the middle hit is worst of all: it leaves two one-segment stubs at $0.250$, indistinguishable by amplitude from an isolated fake.\relax }{figure.caption.7}{}}
\newlabel{eq:ladder}{{10}{13}{Exact activation levels: the atlas}{equation.2.10}{}}
