\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces One zero, and where the surviving errors live. The kept probability is decomposed exactly over the eigenmodes of $A$ and each mode's weight is split into its true (green) and false (red) share by the squared eigenvector components; the histograms show the share of the total kept weight per $0.02$ in $\lambda $, on a log scale. Left: before the filter, under the classical inversion (per-mode weight $\beta _j^{2}/\lambda _j^{2}$). Right: after the one-bit filter (kept weight $\beta _j^{2}\qopname  \relax o{cos}^{2}(\lambda _jt/2)$). Top row: the clean $T=400$ event of figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:clean2x2}; bottom row: the heavy-noise $T=200$ event of figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:pileup}. Dashed: $\lambda =s$; dotted green: the four true-track lines; dotted red and orange: the pair lines $s\pm 1$ and triple lines $s\pm \sqrt  2$. The spike at $\lambda =s$ carries essentially all of the false weight before the filter and none after it; the survivors are the coupled false clusters, and under noise a small share sits on the true lines themselves.\relax }}{22}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:notchspec}{{11}{22}{One zero, and where the surviving errors live. The kept probability is decomposed exactly over the eigenmodes of $A$ and each mode's weight is split into its true (green) and false (red) share by the squared eigenvector components; the histograms show the share of the total kept weight per $0.02$ in $\lambda $, on a log scale. Left: before the filter, under the classical inversion (per-mode weight $\beta _j^{2}/\lambda _j^{2}$). Right: after the one-bit filter (kept weight $\beta _j^{2}\cos ^{2}(\lambda _jt/2)$). Top row: the clean $T=400$ event of figure~\ref {fig:clean2x2}; bottom row: the heavy-noise $T=200$ event of figure~\ref {fig:pileup}. Dashed: $\lambda =s$; dotted green: the four true-track lines; dotted red and orange: the pair lines $s\pm 1$ and triple lines $s\pm \sqrt 2$. The spike at $\lambda =s$ carries essentially all of the false weight before the filter and none after it; the survivors are the coupled false clusters, and under noise a small share sits on the true lines themselves.\relax }{figure.caption.16}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces The chain ladder, on the same analytic footing as figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:atlas} ($\gamma =3$, $\delta =1$, $s=4$, no noise; the 1BQF levels are the readout of eq.\nobreakspace  {}\textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:onebitreadout}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} on the classical scale by the atlas' single global factor). Classical outer activation climbs eq.\nobreakspace  {}\textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:ladder}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} toward $0.366$; the 1BQF pushes the outer level of a five-hit track down to $0.266$ and the middles up to $0.492$, and annihilates the one-segment stub outright, since a lone segment sits exactly on the notch. The marked levels are what a threshold has to clear to keep every segment of a complete track.\relax }}{23}{figure.caption.17}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ladder}{{12}{23}{The chain ladder, on the same analytic footing as figure~\ref {fig:atlas} ($\gamma =3$, $\delta =1$, $s=4$, no noise; the 1BQF levels are the readout of eq.~\eqref {eq:onebitreadout} on the classical scale by the atlas' single global factor). Classical outer activation climbs eq.~\eqref {eq:ladder} toward $0.366$; the 1BQF pushes the outer level of a five-hit track down to $0.266$ and the middles up to $0.492$, and annihilates the one-segment stub outright, since a lone segment sits exactly on the notch. The marked levels are what a threshold has to clear to keep every segment of a complete track.\relax }{figure.caption.17}{}}
