\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24}{\ignorespaces Polynomial degree against the two segment metrics on moderate noise ($T{=}200$, ten repetitions; per-repetition dots, median lines; response refitted at every degree). Stars at the left edge: the matched 1BQF, a degree-one filter, on each operator. (a) Efficiency at a $1\%$ false-rate budget: every operator is flat from $d{\approx }8$ — the response axis is exhausted almost immediately, at $0.975$--$0.978$ on base $A$ against the classical $0.492$. (b) False rate at the matched $99\%$-efficiency point: base $A$ and the fork sit pinned at the $0.31$ twin flood at \emph  {every} degree, while the occupancy operators alone drop to the $10^{-2}$ scale — purity at high efficiency is bought by the operator, not the polynomial.\relax }}{42}{figure.caption.30}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:degmod}{{24}{42}{Polynomial degree against the two segment metrics on moderate noise ($T{=}200$, ten repetitions; per-repetition dots, median lines; response refitted at every degree). Stars at the left edge: the matched 1BQF, a degree-one filter, on each operator. (a) Efficiency at a $1\%$ false-rate budget: every operator is flat from $d{\approx }8$ — the response axis is exhausted almost immediately, at $0.975$--$0.978$ on base $A$ against the classical $0.492$. (b) False rate at the matched $99\%$-efficiency point: base $A$ and the fork sit pinned at the $0.31$ twin flood at \emph {every} degree, while the occupancy operators alone drop to the $10^{-2}$ scale — purity at high efficiency is bought by the operator, not the polynomial.\relax }{figure.caption.30}{}}
