\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {30}{\ignorespaces The figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:clean2x2} pair at the formula acceptance for every series — the classical solver, the 1BQF, and the degree-40 QSVT with coefficients refit on base $A$, $A+$occupancy, $A+$bifurcation, and both — at clean, moderate and heavy noise (rows), $T=200$--$1000$, every solver at its efficiency-first working point. Clean is solved at every multiplicity on every Hamiltonian. Under noise, every curve converges to the false prevalence as the twin flood completes: past the wall the $99\%$-efficiency point stops being a working point at all. The classical solver, read at the same working point, sits at $31\%$--$92\%$ (moderate) and $63\%$--$94\%$ (heavy) false rate over this range.\relax }}{53}{figure.caption.36}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:density5}{{30}{53}{The figure~\ref {fig:clean2x2} pair at the formula acceptance for every series — the classical solver, the 1BQF, and the degree-40 QSVT with coefficients refit on base $A$, $A+$occupancy, $A+$bifurcation, and both — at clean, moderate and heavy noise (rows), $T=200$--$1000$, every solver at its efficiency-first working point. Clean is solved at every multiplicity on every Hamiltonian. Under noise, every curve converges to the false prevalence as the twin flood completes: past the wall the $99\%$-efficiency point stops being a working point at all. The classical solver, read at the same working point, sits at $31\%$--$92\%$ (moderate) and $63\%$--$94\%$ (heavy) false rate over this range.\relax }{figure.caption.36}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {31}{\ignorespaces The clean benchmark of figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:clean2x2} (fixed $\varepsilon =2$\,mrad, $\gamma =3$, no hit drop) with every solver at its efficiency-first working point ($99\%$ of true segments kept; panel (a) shows the convention holding): the classical solver, the 1BQF, and the QSVT (degree 40, base $A$, the clean-event line-comb coefficients; benchmark campaign, mean $\pm $ s.e.m.\ over 3--20 repetitions), together with the same degree-40 QSVT with coefficients refit on base $A$, $A+$occupancy ($\alpha =0.05$), $A+$bifurcation ($\beta =0.5$) and both (repetitions 0--2 of the same events). The line-comb coefficients stop winning once the $2$\,mrad events develop tangles ($T\ge 400$); the refit restores the margin, $2.8\%$ against the classical $10.0\%$ at $T=1000$, on every Hamiltonian. A measured $0\%$ false rate is drawn at the $10^{-3}$ floor.\relax }}{54}{figure.caption.37}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fig6first}{{31}{54}{The clean benchmark of figure~\ref {fig:clean2x2} (fixed $\varepsilon =2$\,mrad, $\gamma =3$, no hit drop) with every solver at its efficiency-first working point ($99\%$ of true segments kept; panel (a) shows the convention holding): the classical solver, the 1BQF, and the QSVT (degree 40, base $A$, the clean-event line-comb coefficients; benchmark campaign, mean $\pm $ s.e.m.\ over 3--20 repetitions), together with the same degree-40 QSVT with coefficients refit on base $A$, $A+$occupancy ($\alpha =0.05$), $A+$bifurcation ($\beta =0.5$) and both (repetitions 0--2 of the same events). The line-comb coefficients stop winning once the $2$\,mrad events develop tangles ($T\ge 400$); the refit restores the margin, $2.8\%$ against the classical $10.0\%$ at $T=1000$, on every Hamiltonian. A measured $0\%$ false rate is drawn at the $10^{-3}$ floor.\relax }{figure.caption.37}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {32}{\ignorespaces The rows of table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:resources} against track count. (a) Logical register width: all constructions follow $\lceil \qopname  \relax o{log}_2 4T^2\rceil $ plus a constant: the 1BQF and DSS--QETU comb lines coincide exactly (the comb pays no width for its degree on this primitive), while the dense-dilation LCU pays $+5$ and the HHL clock band $+7$ to $+9$. (b) Measured two-qubit gate cost per shot: the shared evolution call grows as $\approx T^{1.4}$ (pair count $\times $ ladder length), the three algorithms sitting at fixed multiples of it (2, 40, $\approx 130$--$510$ calls). The green diamonds are the largest single per-cluster instance at each $T$: set by the local cluster size, not by $T$, the flat exception to every other curve's growth.\relax }}{54}{figure.caption.40}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rescmp}{{32}{54}{The rows of table~\ref {tab:resources} against track count. (a) Logical register width: all constructions follow $\lceil \log _2 4T^2\rceil $ plus a constant: the 1BQF and DSS--QETU comb lines coincide exactly (the comb pays no width for its degree on this primitive), while the dense-dilation LCU pays $+5$ and the HHL clock band $+7$ to $+9$. (b) Measured two-qubit gate cost per shot: the shared evolution call grows as $\approx T^{1.4}$ (pair count $\times $ ladder length), the three algorithms sitting at fixed multiples of it (2, 40, $\approx 130$--$510$ calls). The green diamonds are the largest single per-cluster instance at each $T$: set by the local cluster size, not by $T$, the flat exception to every other curve's growth.\relax }{figure.caption.40}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {33}{\ignorespaces The readout budget, measured on the shared events at the production degree $d=40$. (a) Post-selection success probability per repetition: the measured comb points follow $0.44/T$; the 1BQF estimate $0.25/T$ is shown for comparison. (b) Total walk calls per solve after amplitude amplification, following the $d\sqrt  T$ scaling.\relax }}{55}{figure.caption.41}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:readout}{{33}{55}{The readout budget, measured on the shared events at the production degree $d=40$. (a) Post-selection success probability per repetition: the measured comb points follow $0.44/T$; the 1BQF estimate $0.25/T$ is shown for comparison. (b) Total walk calls per solve after amplitude amplification, following the $d\sqrt T$ scaling.\relax }{figure.caption.41}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {34}{\ignorespaces The real-geometry counterpart of the toy's amplitude pile-up: activation-amplitude spectra on real Run-3 VELO event geometry, for the classical solver, the 1BQF, and the redesigned band-plus-notch response. Pink: false segments; orange: true fragments ($m\le 2$); green: findable true segments ($m\ge 3$); the dotted line is each solver's efficiency-first threshold, set so that $99\%$ of the \emph  {findable} true segments (tracks with three or more true segments) survive, with the per-event range shaded; over all true segments the same rule is degenerate, because the $13\%$ of true segments in fragments of one or two segments sit at the isolated level inside the false bulk. The QSVT here is the degree-40 band-limited design on the base Hamiltonian, not the line comb: real tracks have variable length, so the five-hit lines do not apply. Variable track length smears the discrete toy levels into bands, and the fragment population sits inside the false bulk for every solver, the real-geometry form of the same efficiency ceiling.\relax }}{55}{figure.caption.42}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:run3}{{34}{55}{The real-geometry counterpart of the toy's amplitude pile-up: activation-amplitude spectra on real Run-3 VELO event geometry, for the classical solver, the 1BQF, and the redesigned band-plus-notch response. Pink: false segments; orange: true fragments ($m\le 2$); green: findable true segments ($m\ge 3$); the dotted line is each solver's efficiency-first threshold, set so that $99\%$ of the \emph {findable} true segments (tracks with three or more true segments) survive, with the per-event range shaded; over all true segments the same rule is degenerate, because the $13\%$ of true segments in fragments of one or two segments sit at the isolated level inside the false bulk. The QSVT here is the degree-40 band-limited design on the base Hamiltonian, not the line comb: real tracks have variable length, so the five-hit lines do not apply. Variable track length smears the discrete toy levels into bands, and the fragment population sits inside the false bulk for every solver, the real-geometry form of the same efficiency ceiling.\relax }{figure.caption.42}{}}
